WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.620
Peace be upon you and the mercy of God and His blessings. Welcome to the second video of the second module of our fellowship. In this explanation, we will enter directly into very interesting details.

00:08.620 --> 00:15.420
We are always accustomed to looking at health as a doctor's prescription or a medicine pill that we take and say: "Oh Opener, all-Knowing."

00:15.600 --> 00:21.780
Yet the reality is much deeper than that. In this analysis, we will uncover the hidden roots that shape our health without us realizing it.

00:22.120 --> 00:27.680
We will understand how health is not just a personal decision. No. It is a complex network of interconnected circumstances.

00:27.680 --> 00:41.800
Let us enter the subject: Imagine with me this scene: Two neighbors living on the same street, same age, same air. One of them, God willing, his health is like iron, athletic, moving actively and energetically every day as if appearing in a vitamin commercial.

00:42.100 --> 00:50.760
And the second is completely the opposite: His health is in decline, always catching the flu, tired, spending his time as a regular customer in clinics, meaning the neighborhood pharmacy opens in the morning just for him.

00:50.760 --> 01:03.740
We see this scene often, and this contrast raises an important question: Is this strange disparity merely genetic luck? Meaning one had good luck and the other drew a losing card? Or is the story about the doctors' skill?

01:03.740 --> 01:17.900
The truth is that restricting health to genetics or doctors does injustice to reality. The story is not just a surgeon's scalpel or a genetic tape we were born with. The story is hidden forces that play the leading role in our health, which in science we call the determinants of health.

01:17.900 --> 01:20.740
In brief, these are the conditions and environments that we descend into.

01:20.760 --> 01:50.760
We are born, grow, work, and age in them. Not medical decisions inside the examination room. We are talking about a complex fabric of circumstances, most of them outside our direct control, yet shaping our lives every day. To understand this fabric, let us unpack each layer: The first layer: Biology and individual behavior. The beginning is always from genetics, age, sex, and family medical history. For example, if diabetes runs in the family, this is a genetic predisposition that increases the chance. But focus with me: Genetic predisposition is not a final death sentence. The gene exists and sleeps, waiting for something to wake it up. And here comes the role of our habits: food, drinking water, sleep quality, exercise, smoking. These behaviors are what control genetic risks. But even these habits do not occur in a vacuum. It is not that a person suddenly wakes up in the morning and decides to become a healthy green creature running in the street and drinking celery smoothies, if the surrounding environment does not support them in the first place. And this leads us to the second layer: Environment and social level. The environment and social class are what determine whether healthy habits are an accessible option or an impossible luxury.

01:50.760 --> 02:20.760
One plays in a green park, breathing pure air in a quiet countryside. And the second has his home on a highway next to two factories, breathing car exhaust and smoke day and night. Meaning the second child has carbon for breakfast and street nicotine for dinner. The second child's chance of getting asthma is not bad luck from him; this is an environmental challenge imposed on him by force. Add to that noise, water purity, and housing quality. And on top of all this: income and education. Income and education are like the strict building doormen: They determine your ability to live in a clean place, buy fresh food, and receive treatment without debt. And someone with higher education has deeper health awareness, while economic pressures place suffocating constraints on families. To see how these determinants interact in reality, let us hear the story of two diabetic patients: Both diagnosed on the same day with the exact same disease. The first patient's income is excellent. He lives in a neighborhood with a high-end supermarket selling fresh fruits and vegetables like gold coins, and at the beginning of the street there is a health center following up on him easily. This person will control his blood sugar and live with the illness with complete peace of mind. The second patient is broke, living in a remote area with nothing except small shops selling biscuits and chips, requiring two public transit rides and an hour and twenty minutes of exhaustion to reach the nearest clinic for an HbA1c test. Is the deterioration of the second patient's condition caused by the disease itself? Certainly not. Economic and environmental conditions are what tipped the scale and built a dam in front of him.

02:20.760 --> 02:50.760
And now let us widen the lens a little and reach the greatest threat: Climate change, the amplifier of threats and burdens. Climate change is not merely temperature degrees on news screens, or people feeling a bit warm and wanting an ice pop. It strikes crops and food security, reduces drinking water quality, and increases air pollution. Meaning at the very same time that your home becomes stiflingly hot and suffocating, basic resources are hit, and the environment turns into an environment completely hostile to health. That is why defending the environment and climate is not prestige or a luxury for activists wearing wool sweaters; this is a direct defense of our preventive health and our families' bodies.

02:50.760 --> 03:20.760
This brings us to the core point: Empathy first, and the mission of the health educator. Health education separated from reality is useless. We frequently hear advice such as: "You must eat an avocado bowl with chia seeds daily and completely avoid psychological stress." Oh, what sweetness! Globally, this advice is wonderful and 100 percent correct. But realistically, the price of an avocado alone brings the citizen psychological stress and a three-dimensional stroke! It is very easy to give top-down orders. But the smart and humane health educator asks themselves first: Is this healthy food within reach in the first place? What are the financial circumstances that trapped this person? This empathy makes you formulate messages that touch the ground and genuinely help people overcome challenges.

03:20.760 --> 03:50.760
And in conclusion, we leave you with this question for reflection: What is the most impactful hidden factor on people's health in your community today? Is it income? Environmental or housing quality? Or ease of access to healthcare services? Thinking about the answer to this question is what will pave the way for our upcoming video, in which we will translate these facts into practical tools to confront climate crises in our Arab region. Thank you, and we will see you in the next video.
